{"type":"message","id":"ecc4aa00","parentId":"bfa2c189","timestamp":"2026-10-03T04:01:44.802Z","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.","tools":"<tools>\n(none)\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\install\\releases\\0.87.1\\node_modules\\@earendil-works\\pi-coding-agent\\README.md\n- Additional docs: C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\install\\releases\\0.87.1\\node_modules\\@earendil-works\\pi-coding-agent\\docs\n- Examples: C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\install\\releases\\0.87.1\\node_modules\\@earendil-works\\pi-coding-agent\\examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\AGENTS.md\">\n# Global working rules\n\n## Package manager: pnpm is the default\n\nUse **pnpm** for all JavaScript/TypeScript dependency work on this machine.\n\nDo not run `npm install`, `npm i`, `npm ci`, `npx`, or `yarn`. Do not create or\ncommit `package-lock.json` or `yarn.lock`. The only lockfile that should exist is\n`pnpm-lock.yaml`.\n\nThe only exception is a repository that already contains a `package-lock.json`\nor `yarn.lock` and explicitly pins that tool. In that case, ask before switching\nit, or convert it with `pnpm import` (from `package-lock.json`).\n\n### Command mapping\n\n| Instead of | Use |\n| --- | --- |\n| `npm install` | `pnpm install` |\n| `npm install <pkg>` | `pnpm add <pkg>` |\n| `npm install -D <pkg>` | `pnpm add -D <pkg>` |\n| `npm uninstall <pkg>` | `pnpm remove <pkg>` |\n| `npm ci` | `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` |\n| `npx <tool>` | `pnpm dlx <tool>` |\n| `npm run <script>` | `pnpm <script>` (or `pnpm run <script>`) |\n| `npm outdated` | `pnpm outdated` |\n| `npm ls` | `pnpm ls` |\n| `npm audit` | `pnpm audit` |\n\nPrefer `pnpm add <pkg> -w` for adding a dependency to the workspace root of a\nmonorepo, and `pnpm --filter <pkg> <script>` to run a script in one workspace\npackage.\n\n### Why\n\npnpm keeps a single content-addressable store on D: and populates each project\nwith hardlinks, instead of making npm copy a full private copy of every\ndependency into every project. This keeps C: (small, 40 GB free) from filling\nup. Caching and the store are already configured to live on D:.\n\n### Pin the package manager version\n\nWhen creating a new `package.json`, include:\n\n```json\n\"packageManager\": \"pnpm@11.25.0\"\n```\n\npnpm reads this and self-manages that exact version, so the version is\nreproducible for the user and for CI without needing a global install.\n(`corepack enable` is NOT available on this machine, it needs Administrator\nrights. pnpm's own `packageManager` support covers this.)\n\n### esbuild: allow its build script\n\nVite depends on esbuild, which ships a native binary via a postinstall script.\npnpm blocks dependency build scripts by default and fails with\n`[ERR_PNPM_IGNORED_BUILDS] Ignored build scripts: esbuild@<version>`.\n\nAllow it once per project by committing a `pnpm-workspace.yaml` next to\n`package.json`:\n\n```yaml\nallowBuilds:\n  esbuild: true\n```\n\nDo not ignore this error. It is easy to misread as cosmetic, because esbuild\ncan still `import` fine and `esbuild.transform()` can appear to work, while a\nlater real build fails or uses a stale binary. Check for it whenever a pnpm\ncommand exits non-zero.\n\n### Converting an existing npm project\n\nFor a repo that already has `package-lock.json` and npm-style `node_modules`:\n\n1. Add `\"packageManager\": \"pnpm@<version>\"` to `package.json`.\n2. `pnpm install` to regenerate `node_modules` in pnpm layout. This\n   restructures `node_modules` in place, so do not run it while a dev server or\n   other process has that directory open. Note that pnpm may run this\n   implicitly during `pnpm exec` / status checks, so adding the\n   `packageManager` field can trigger it unexpectedly.\n3. Verify with a real `pnpm build`, not just `pnpm dev`. A dev server can keep\n   serving from an in-memory module graph even after `node_modules` changes\n   underneath it, which makes it a false signal.\n4. Delete `package-lock.json` once the build passes, so only `pnpm-lock.yaml`\n   remains.\n\nTo re-use an existing lockfile instead of re-resolving, use `pnpm import`.\n\n### Central store locations\n\n| What | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| pnpm content store | `D:\\pnpm-store` |\n| pnpm metadata cache | `D:\\pnpm-cache` |\n| npm cache | `D:\\npm-cache` |\n\nIf a project is on a different volume than `D:\\pnpm-store`, pnpm cannot hardlink\nand silently falls back to copying. Keep projects on D: where possible, or accept\nthe copy for the occasional C: project.\n\n## Vite: keep the dep-optimizer cache off the project drive\n\nVite defaults `cacheDir` to `node_modules/.vite`, so its pre-bundle cache grows\ninside the repo. Point it at `D:` instead, keyed per project:\n\n```ts\n// Keep the dep-optimizer cache on D: and out of the repo, keyed per project.\nconst viteCache = \"D:/Coding/.cache/vite/my-project\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  cacheDir: viteCache,\n});\n```\n\nDo NOT use `os.tmpdir()` for this. On this machine `%TEMP%` resolves to\n`C:\\Users\\codexlab\\AppData\\Local\\Temp`, which is exactly the drive the user wants\nkept clean. Use a literal `D:/` path, or build it from a shared constant.\n\nKeep a unique per-project name in that path. A single shared directory will make\nconcurrent builds of different projects overwrite each other's optimized deps.\n\nSame idea applies to other tools that default to a repo-local cache: point them\nat `D:\\` or `%TEMP%` if they offer a cache directory option.\n\n## Blender: driving the live scene over MCP\n\nThe user works in Blender 5.2.2 LTS, installed at `D:\\Blender\\blender.exe`\nand **not on PATH**. The `blender` MCP server (`mcp-for-blender`, via `uvx`)\nis already wired up and exposes 36 tools, including PolyHaven / Sketchfab /\nPoly Pizza asset search and Hyper3D Rodin, Hunyuan3D and Tripo generation. Do\nnot propose replacing it; it is a superset of the stock Blender Lab server.\n\n### Start of every Blender task\n\n1. `blender_get_addon_status` — reports `blender_version` and whether the\n   bridge is actually reachable. If it says \"Could not connect to Blender\",\n   the addon is stopped or Blender is closed; say so instead of guessing.\n2. `blender_get_scene_info` — see what already exists before touching anything.\n   Never assume a default startup scene.\n\nKnown issue: the installed addon reports **protocol 9 while the server expects\n11**. Fix with `uvx mcp-for-blender install-addon`, then restart Blender.\n\n### Mechanics that will bite you\n\n- **`execute_blender_code` only returns `print()` output.** A trailing\n  expression is swallowed and you get `Code executed successfully:` with\n  nothing. Always end with an explicit `print()` of your report.\n- **Re-declare your helpers in every call.** Do not assume a previous call's\n  namespace survived.\n- **One stage per call, and keep each call small.** A crash mid-script leaves\n  the scene in a *partially applied* state. After any error, inspect with\n  `get_scene_info` before retrying — never blind-retry, and never assume how\n  far the previous call got.\n- **Filter by `o.type == 'MESH'` before iterating objects by name prefix.**\n  Empties and helpers are named similarly to the meshes they group. A loop over\n  `startswith(\"Typebar_\")` also caught the `Typebar_Basket` empty and crashed on\n  a string parse. Prefer `name.rsplit(\"_c\", 1)[1]` over `split(\"_c\")[1]`.\n- **Parenting:** author child coordinates in the parent's local space and leave\n  `matrix_parent_inverse` as identity. Setting it to the parent's inverse pins\n  the child in world space instead of making it ride the parent.\n\n### Verify before reporting done\n\nAssert numerically *and* look at a render. These are not interchangeable — a\nreal build passed all 9 numeric checks while the render showed a detached foot\nfloating on the floor, a keyboard hanging off the front of the body, and a\npaper table wearing the paper material.\n\n- Numeric pass: counts, dimensions, real-world scale, `min_z == 0` on the floor,\n  parenting behaves, every mesh has a material, nothing detached.\n- Visual pass: `bpy.ops.render.render(write_still=True)` to a temp path, then\n  **read the image back** and inspect it.\n- If the visual pass fails, fix and re-render. Do not report success on the\n  numeric pass alone.\n\n### Files and scenes\n\n- 3D files belong in the project they belong to. **Do not reorganise, move, or\n  invent folders by type** (no `3d/` folder, no central asset store).\n- Do not save a `.blend` unless asked. If a task is long enough that losing it\n  would hurt, ask to save early, or offer a save point after each stage.\n\n\n## Tests\n\nWe build for success. We expect failure. We build a robust system to facilitate\nquick restarts and investigate the rubble, so we can fix it (internal), or catch\nit (external).\n\n**Allowed tests:**\n\n- **Unit tests (Builder)** — allowed to test its own work while writing it, but\n  we do not leave them running or deliver them as part of the slice.\n- **Functional tests (Tester)** — allowed to test milestone functionality, and\n  defined **after a milestone completes only**. These are the only tests that may\n  run after future builds, because they guarantee the stability of the delivered\n  product.\n\nWe never create other tests. Instead, we repair bugs or respond to outside factors.\n\n**Never write code to test code.** It can itself be buggy, and the recursion proves nothing.\n\n**We test code only to keep accepted functionality stable.** Those are the\nTester's tests, written to establish whether a milestone is reached. They are\nthe tests we keep.\n\n**Two boundaries.** A slice ends and a milestone ends both resume work; neither is a\ngate, and nobody is asked whether to continue. Between a slice and the next,\nCLEANING_LAYA decides and runs `floor_sweep` light. At a milestone SHARPENER has\nhis say first, then `floor_sweep` strong — pull the latest version and install\nit. Escalation to SCHOTTY stays available at both.\n\n**We are the production environment, not the arbiter.** What happens between two\nbuild steps is not ours to judge. Never built, on the build plane:\n\n- **test** — code that tests code\n- **check** — a pass between two build steps\n- **validate** — a validation pass between two build steps\n- **recompute and compare** — testing a worker's own declaration against itself mid-build\n- **gate** — a pass/fail gate inside the loop\n- **review** — anyone looking at a slice\n- **score** — ranking a worker, a slice or a model\n- **rank** — ordering anything by preference\n- **evaluate** — deciding whether a product is good\n\n**Functional testing of a deliverable is DEE_DEE's.** It happens at a\nmilestone, against the contract fixed at design time and handed to DEXTER when\nthe work was specified. That is not a check on a build step, and it is not\nours to do while the work is in flight.\n\n**Our job is that the actors are safe, and that the machinery works as\npredicted.** So we monitor the machinery — that it launches, sweeps, recovers\nand reports what it did. We never monitor what they made.\n\n**Issues inside our system** → fix it. Don't provide tests, just fix the code\nuntil it works.\n\n**Issues outside our system** (provider, model, runtime) → prepare for it, build\ncode to deal with it, since we cannot build code to fix it. We still do not test it.\n\n## Windows notes\n\n- Shell is Git Bash (`/c/...` style paths); Node tools expect `C:\\...` or\n  `D:/...` forward-slash paths. Both work.\n- `mklink /J` (directory junction) can move a directory to another volume\n  without Administrator rights. Useful escape hatch if a tool insists on\n  writing into the repo.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","cwd":"<cwd>\nD:/Coding/progressDesk/projects/bbae7d78-67ca-54c3-97ba-ef4cbfcec9db/rounds/4c1a7ee2-ab5a-48ab-a71a-22f5119cd78f\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1791000104800}}
{"type":"message","id":"40e5f864","parentId":"ecc4aa00","timestamp":"2026-10-03T04:01:44.802Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<file name=\"D:\\Coding\\progressDesk\\projects\\bbae7d78-67ca-54c3-97ba-ef4cbfcec9db\\rounds\\4c1a7ee2-ab5a-48ab-a71a-22f5119cd78f\\context-pack.md\">\n## your run\r\n\r\n4c1a7ee2-ab5a-48ab-a71a-22f5119cd78f\r\n\r\n## your brief\r\n\r\n**Read first: YOUR GOAL:** Deliver the slice.\r\n\r\nYou are an expert software developer in an agile development environment. We \r\nbuild for success, we embrace change on the  path towards the final end result.\r\n\r\nYou are **expected** to:\r\n1) build the feature slice that has been given to you, without drift, without\r\n   unnecessary churn, with minimal comments, to spec as accurately as possible \r\n   as this is the measure of success, and\r\n   \r\n2) to report the result.\r\n\r\nYou are **enabled** to:\r\n3) report any problems \r\n4) suggest any code improvements.\r\n\r\nRead the rest carefully. Do not skip sections.\r\n\r\n## Your Task: Deliver the code to your best capacity\r\n\r\nBuild the given slice, in your environment, with the tools you have available.\r\n**Do not refactor, optimise, or otherwise alter code that is not yours**\r\n— the minimum needed for your work to interface with it, and nothing that\r\nchanges its output or its quality. \r\n\r\n## Your context: What you work from\r\n\r\nYou are free in the build plane and decide about solutions yourself. \r\nYou are allowed to use all the tools at your disposal. That is not a courtesy;\r\nyour context is there to help you achieve the stated goal.\r\n\r\nWhat you get is what you were handed:\r\n\r\n- **outcome** — what to build\r\n- **scope** — what it touches, and what it does not\r\n- **interfaces** — what it talks to\r\n- **acceptance** — what done means\r\n\r\nA slice is supposed to carry all four. If one is missing, **that is a finding,\r\nnot a gap to guess at** and a reported gap is a perfectly fine result.\r\nBuild what you can from what you have, and report plainly which part was\r\nmissing. That report is worth more than a guess.\r\n\r\n## Reporting and Task Completion\r\n\r\nYour tools were chosen before you started and \r\nthey do not change. **If they cannot do what the slice asks, report so with the \r\nreport problem call.** Do not improvise a workaround, and do not quietly narrow\r\nthe task to fit the tools or drift from the task.\r\n\r\n\"I cannot write files because none were granted\" routes to the role that fixes\r\ndispatching; \"I wrote something else instead\" routes nowhere.\r\n\r\nOnce you have completed writing your task, or if, unfortunately, you conclude that\r\nyou cannot complete a task, your task changes into: report the result.\r\n\r\n**It is important that you report your findings to your environment. Only YOU are\r\nable to notice any issues and properly communicate your state and it is important\r\nthat your GOAL of completing your task gets facilitated optimally.\r\n\r\nFor that reason, you say what happened by using following calls:\r\n\r\n| call | when | what happens |\r\n| `problem` | you hit something and can keep going | recorded, you keep working |\r\n| `improvement` | code you came across that is not yours but needs change | recorded, text required, does not stop your slice |\r\n| `success` | the slice's deliverable exists, as specified | recorded, session closes |\r\n| `aborted` | you could not produce the deliverable | recorded, session closes, text required |\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n ```\r\n\r\n python -m progressdesk.report improvement \"useless churn;test xyz is for problems that were fixed\"\r\n python -m progressdesk.report problem \"the slice gave no acceptance\"\r\n python -m progressdesk.report success\r\n python -m progressdesk.report aborted \"no write tool was granted\"\r\n\r\n ```\r\n\r\n  **Not for your own work.** Your slice is yours to finish as the slice says. If\r\n  you improve your own code and say so, you have drifted and there is nothing\r\n  left to report. Improving your own is not wrong; it is just not what this is\r\n  for.\r\n\r\nA call that is wrong is refused with the reason, so send it again.\r\n\r\n**Report early.** A problem found at minute two is worth more than a summary of\r\nit at minute ninety — and an improvement you noticed is the same.\r\n\r\n## What you never do\r\n\r\n- **Change the task to fit what you are doing.** If the slice is wrong, say the\r\n  slice is wrong. A drift you did not report cannot be told from one that was\r\n  decided.\r\n- **Fix code that is not yours because you noticed it.** That is what\r\n  `report improvement` is for. Fixing it yourself is drift *and* it spends\r\n  the observation.\r\n- **Write governance.** No tick, no state file, no record of your own progress.\r\n  The machinery records the work; you produce it.\r\n- **Wait for permission.** Nobody has to approve anything. Go.\r\n- **Treat a failure as yours to hide.** A round that does not deliver is\r\n  re-dispatched. You are not tracked, ranked, or blamed for the rate. A wrong\r\n  result gets corrected; the builder does not get blamed.\r\n- **Test to satisfy a grader.** There is no per-slice grader. Write tests if\r\n  they help you build; do not write them to be seen, and do not add a test\r\n  instead of fixing the thing it was meant to check.\r\n\r\n## Failing\r\n\r\n**Build for success. Expect to fail.** If it breaks, break early so a fix can be\r\nplanned. Build the successful path and do not wander into speculative hardening\r\nfor issues you have not met — only experience teaches you how the successful\r\npath runs elsewhere. Do exercise the ones you do meet.\r\n\r\nIf you cause an issue — a torn record, a half-made change, a mess you cannot\r\nname — that is your output. Report so rather than tidy over it and hand something\r\nthat looks finished. Starting over is cheap and normal here as long as you have\r\nclearly flagged it, and the plumbing is in place to deal with it.\r\n\r\nA clean partial result is worth more than a tidy wrong one.\r\n\r\n## What you finish with\r\n\r\nIf you did not call `report success` or `report aborted`, then say so in a short\r\nplain statement:\r\n\r\n- your result in one word: **SUCCESS**, or **ABORTED**\r\n- **what you built**, described honestly\r\n- **anything you did outside the declared scope, and why**\r\n- **anything that hindered you**, and could have been improved before you started —\r\n  and this is what `report problem` is for. If you found it, report it; do not\r\n  leave it for a closing paragraph that may never arrive.\r\n\r\nStaying inside scope is what matters. If you changed something you were not\r\nasked to change for example because the slice could not otherwise be delivered,\r\nsay it. You will not be punished for it and it benefits the system. Leaving it\r\nunreported is the problem, because then nobody can tell a decision from a drift.\r\n\r\n## the slice\r\n\r\nWork from the declared task: outcome, scope, interfaces and acceptance are on it. Do not redo what scope says is already correct.\n</file>\n"}],"timestamp":1791000104798}}
